<section class="home-section">
  <h2>Name the intent, not the value.</h2>
  <p>A palette step tells you what a color is. A semantic token tells you what it’s for.
    Change your palette or switch themes without rewriting every component.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-css">:root {
  /* Your primitives */
  --neutral-950: #18242b;
  --neutral-50: #f7fafb;

  /* Shared semantic names */
  --text-primary: var(--neutral-950);
  --bg-main: var(--neutral-50);
}

.card {
  color: var(--text-primary);
  background: var(--bg-main);
}</code></pre>
  <p class="note">Values shown here are illustrative. Argot supplies the vocabulary; you supply the design.</p>
</section>
<section class="home-section">
  <h2>One layer of your design system.</h2>
  <div class="tier-grid">
    <article><p class="eyebrow">01 / Primitive</p><h3>The raw materials</h3><p>Your palette, font families, and static values. <code>--neutral-950</code></p></article>
    <article><p class="eyebrow">02 / Semantic</p><h3>Where Argot fits</h3><p>Shared roles for those values, independent of components. <code>--text-primary</code></p></article>
    <article><p class="eyebrow">03 / Component</p><h3>The local decisions</h3><p>Optional properties scoped to individual components. <code>--card-color</code></p></article>
